{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"eb8916ce777ccb9c","type":"text","text":"- used to provide both authentication and encryption of wireless traffic\n- For encryption, WEP uses RC4 algorithm\n- WEP is a 'shared key' protocol, requiring the sender and receiver to have the same key.\n- WEP keys can be 40 bits or 104 bits in length\n- The bove keys are combined with a 24 'IV' (initialization vector) to bring the total length to 64 bits or 128 bits\n- WEP encryption is not secure and can easily be cracked.\n- WEP can be used for authentication like this:","x":-1590,"y":533,"width":548,"height":279},
		{"id":"0fd5e58b483cf92b","type":"text","text":"WEP","x":-1590,"y":475,"width":548,"height":58,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f5556fb31e736fa7","type":"text","text":"Open Authentication","x":-2180,"y":475,"width":470,"height":58,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ef2bc2fab56509b4","type":"text","text":"- The client sends an authentication request, and the AP accepts it. No questions asked!\n- Not a secure method\n- After the client is authenticated and associated with the AP, it's possible to require the  user to authenticate via other methods before access to the network is granted ","x":-2180,"y":533,"width":470,"height":279},
		{"id":"ab1b274db66856a2","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805173916.png","x":-1590,"y":812,"width":548,"height":87},
		{"id":"5e7f7f3514a8aaea","type":"text","text":"LEAP","x":-2222,"y":1267,"width":400,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"22991c904b0b21bf","type":"text","text":"- Lightweight EAP\n- developed by Cisco as an improvement over WEP\n- Clients must provide a username and password to authenticate\n- In addition, mutual authentication is provided by both the client and server sending a challenge phrase to each other\n- Dynamic WEP keys are used, meaning that the WEP keys are changed frequently. Like WEP, LEAP, is considered vulnerable and should not be used anymore.\n- Like WEP, LEAP is considered vulnerable and should not be used anymore.","x":-2222,"y":1327,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"74904faeba0fd726","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805170638.png","x":-2222,"y":1727,"width":400,"height":166},
		{"id":"b3c3e65f61fd5b0c","type":"text","text":"EAP-FAST","x":-1752,"y":1268,"width":400,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"de663b86640e8076","type":"text","text":"- EAP-FAST was also developed by Cisco\n- Consists of three phases:\n\t- 1) A PAC (Protected Access Credential) is generated and passed from the server to the client.\n\t- 2) A secure TLS tunnel is established between the client and authentication server\n\t- 3) Inside of the secure (encrypted) TLS tunnel, the client and server communicate further to authenticate/authorize the client","x":-1752,"y":1328,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"e62ed4458a4a94ae","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805171249.png","x":-1752,"y":1728,"width":399,"height":165},
		{"id":"aae1126167a8c072","type":"text","text":"PEAP (Protected EAP)","x":-1272,"y":1267,"width":400,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"866d616c9f9dec74","type":"text","text":"- Like EAP-FAST, PEAP involved establishing a secure TLS tunnel betwwen the client and server.\n- Instead of a PAC, the server has a digital certificate.\n- The client uses this digital certificate to authenticate the server.\n- The certficiate is also used to establish a TLS tunnel\n- Because only the server provides a certificate for authentication, the client must still be authenticated within the secure tunnel, for example by using MS-CHAP (Microsoft Challenge-Handshake Authentication Prorocol)","x":-1272,"y":1327,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"1f70def578b90206","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805171555.png","x":-1272,"y":1727,"width":399,"height":158},
		{"id":"2b985d2af7b61391","type":"text","text":"EAP-TLS (EAP Transport Layer Security)","x":-762,"y":1267,"width":400,"height":60,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"1cebab7460c9030e","type":"text","text":"- Whereas PEAP only requires the AS to have a certificate, EAP-TLS requires a certificate on the AS and on every single client\n- EAP-TLS is the most secure wireless authentication method, but is more difficult to implement than PEAP because every client device needs a certificate.\n- Because the client and server authenticate each other with digital certificates, there is no need to authenticate teh client within the TLS tunnel.\n- The TLS tunnel is still used to exchange encryption key information (encryption methods will be discussed next!)","x":-762,"y":1327,"width":400,"height":400},
		{"id":"beae4fd4b1179a95","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805172056.png","x":-762,"y":1727,"width":399,"height":163},
		{"id":"1947887103ef5112","type":"text","text":"EAP Authentication methods (AES Methods)","x":-1474,"y":1000,"width":285,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"f285ad9edc21d2bd","type":"text","text":"methods","x":-1439,"y":269,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"11be93f00f695d84","type":"text","text":"Authentication","x":-1519,"y":-500,"width":410,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"e2b5441df773cbd2","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805173420.png","x":-1519,"y":-10,"width":409,"height":199},
		{"id":"0613485bda435fe8","type":"text","text":"- All clients must be authenticated before they can associate with an AP.\n- In a corporate setting, only trusted users/devices should be given access to the network.\n\t- in corporate settings, a separate SSID which doesn't have access to the corporate network can be provided for guest users.\n- Ideally, clients should also authenticate the AP to avoid associating with a malicious AP.\n- There are multiple ways to authenticate:\n\t- password\n\t- Username/password\n\t- Certificates","x":-1519,"y":-440,"width":410,"height":430},
		{"id":"b56394617566723d","type":"text","text":"TKIP (Temporal Key Integrity Protocol)","x":-80,"y":637,"width":552,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"4bee14e31fd0ee71","type":"text","text":"CCMP (Counter/CBC-MAC Protocol)","x":572,"y":637,"width":550,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"857581987b4673f9","type":"text","text":"- WEP was found to be vulnerablem but wireless hardware at the time was built to use WEP\n- A temporary solution was needed until a new standard was created and new hardware was built.\n- TKIP adds various security features:\n\n## Features\n- A MIC is aded to protect the integrity of messages.\n- A key mixing algorithm i used to create a unique WEP key for every frame\n- The initialization vector is double in length from 24 bits to 48 bits, making brute-force attacks much more difficult\n- The MIC includes the sender MAC address to identify the frame's sender\n- A timestamp is aded to the MIC to precent replay attacks. REplay attacks involve re-resending a frame that has already been transmitted.\n- A TKIP sequence number is used to keep track of frames sent from each source MAC address. This also protects against replay attacks.\n\n## Things to memorize\n- TKIP is used in WPA version 1\n- It was a band-aid solution for WEP\n","x":-80,"y":697,"width":552,"height":703},
		{"id":"b121a40fae86f85d","type":"text","text":"Encryption","x":102,"y":-483,"width":398,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"30681beef826e585","type":"text","text":"Integrity","x":1000,"y":-483,"width":398,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0b2c51e75294397","type":"text","text":"- scrambled the message so that it can't be read by anyone except the AP and the client\n- There are many possible protocols that can be used to encrypt traffic\n- All devices on the WLAN will use the same protocol, however each client will use a unique encryption/decryption key so that other devices can't read its traffic.\n- A 'group key' is used by the AP to encrypt traffic it wants to send to all of its clients. \n\t- All of the clients associated with the AP keep that key so they can decrypt the traffic","x":102,"y":-423,"width":398,"height":387},
		{"id":"2a6b712f7d87a5b7","type":"text","text":"- Ensures that a message is not modified by a third-party. The message that is sent by the source host should be the same as the message that is received by the destination host\n- A MIC (message integrity check) is added to messaged to help protect their integrity","x":1000,"y":-423,"width":398,"height":387},
		{"id":"12bcff824e836706","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805174519.png","x":1000,"y":-36,"width":400,"height":225},
		{"id":"6df19fe7c982f1d9","type":"text","text":"- CCMP was developed after TKIP and is more secure.\n- It is used in WPA2 \n- To use CCMP, it must be supported by the device's hardware. Old hardware built only to use WEP/TKIP cannot use CCMP. \n- CCMP consists of two different algorithms to provide encryption and MIC\n\t- 1) AES (Advanced Encryption Standard) counter mode encryption \n\t\t- AES is the most secure encryption protocol currently available. It is widely used all over the world. \n\t\t- There are multiple modes of operation for AES. CCMP uses 'counter mode'.\n\t- 2) CBC-MAC (Cipher Block Chaining Message Authentication Code) is used as a mic to ensure the integrity of messages.\n\t- ","x":572,"y":697,"width":552,"height":703},
		{"id":"e5b0a018f09dca84","type":"text","text":"GCMP (Galois/Counter Mode Protocol)","x":1260,"y":637,"width":552,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"68a8b42c4fc51fe7","type":"text","text":"- GCMP is more secure and efficient than CCMP\n- its increased efficiency allows higher data throughput than CCMP\n- It is used in WPA3\n- GCMP consists of two algorithms\n\t- 1) AES counter mode encrpytion\n\t- 2) GMAC (Galois Message Authentication Code) is used as a MIC to ensure the integrity of messages","x":1260,"y":697,"width":552,"height":703},
		{"id":"b02f5a00e18c370c","type":"text","text":"Encrpytion and Integrity methods","x":680,"y":400,"width":320,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"e4441395f663721c","type":"text","text":"## TKIP\n- based on WEP, but more secure.\n- Should not be used in modern networks\n- WPA\n\n## CCMP\n- AES counter mode for encrpytion\n- CMC-MAC for MIC\n- WPA2\n## GCMP\n- AES counter mode for encryption\n- GMAC for MIC\n- WPA3","x":570,"y":1577,"width":552,"height":523},
		{"id":"dd36cead5ee6ec68","type":"text","text":"Summary","x":570,"y":1527,"width":552,"height":50,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"0bb44a61c1439abe","type":"text","text":"WPA","x":2160,"y":77,"width":398,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"4b643aba1fecf529","type":"text","text":"WPA2","x":2620,"y":77,"width":398,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"981b7c5a83356bcf","type":"text","text":"WPA3","x":3080,"y":77,"width":398,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"f1ec7586a4153f55","type":"text","text":"The WPA certification was developed after WEP was proven to be vulnerable and includes the following protocols:\n- TKIP (based on WEP) provides encryption/MIC\n- 802.1X authentication(Enterprise mode) or PSK (personal mode)","x":2160,"y":137,"width":398,"height":483},
		{"id":"d88e75f5759864f6","type":"text","text":"WPA2 was released in 2004 and includes the following protocols:\n- CCMP provides encryption/MIC\n- 802.1X authenticatoin (Enterprise mode) or PSK (personal mode)","x":2620,"y":137,"width":398,"height":483},
		{"id":"83c9524306b5ad70","type":"text","text":"- WPA3 was released in 2018 and includes the following protocols:\n\t- GCMP provides encryption/MIC\n\t- 802.1X authentication (enterprise mode) or PSK (personal mode)\n\t- WPA3 also provides serveral additional security features, for example:\n- PMF (Protected Management Frames), protecting 802.11 management frame from eavesdropping/forging\n- SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals) protects the four-way handshake when using personal mode authentication\n- Forward Secrecy prevents data from being decrypted after it has been transmitted over the air. So, an attacker can't capture wireless frames and then try to decrypt them later","x":3080,"y":137,"width":398,"height":483},
		{"id":"1c024053ed539a87","x":2480,"y":-583,"width":680,"height":52,"color":"5","type":"text","text":"WiFi Protected Access"},
		{"id":"84b2019f23d47740","type":"text","text":"- The Wi-Fi alliance has developed three WPA certifications for wireless devices:\n\t- WPA\n\t- WPA2\n\t- WPA3\n- To be WPA-certified, equipment must be tested in authorized testing labs\n- All of the above support two authentication modes:\n\t- Personal mode: A pre-shared key (PSK) us used for authentication. When you connect to a home Wi-Fi network, enter the password and are authenticated, that is personal mode.\n\t\t- This is common in small networks\n\t\t- The PSK itself is not sent over the air. A four-way handshake is used for authentication, and the PSK is used to generate encryption keys.\n\t- Enterprise mode\n\t\t- 802.1x is used with an authentication server (RADIUS server). No Specific EAP method is specified, so all are suported (PEAP, EAP-TLS, etc.)","x":2480,"y":-531,"width":677,"height":451},
		{"id":"8e1453fc3d8aca01","type":"text","text":"Wireless Security","x":1074,"y":-1440,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"7861695dc0d116d8","type":"text","text":"- is an authentication framework\n- Identifies a standard set of authentication functions that are used by the various EAP Methods\n\t- LEAP, EAP-FAST, PEAP, EAP-TLS\n- EAP is integrated with 802.1X, which provides port-based network access control","x":-920,"y":529,"width":400,"height":240},
		{"id":"7fff234a044102da","type":"file","file":"zPictures/Pasted image 20260805170338.png","x":-920,"y":769,"width":400,"height":144},
		{"id":"d800d37318882f9c","type":"text","text":"EAP (Extensible Authentication Protocol)","x":-920,"y":479,"width":400,"height":50,"color":"4"}
	],
